// ============================================================================
// divisor-count.epher -- how many divisors a number has, tau(n)
//
// Prints the ndivisors builtin for a few numbers. The count is the
// divisor function tau(n), and the formula behind it is the point of the
// comments: if n = p^a * q^b * r^c with distinct primes, then every
// divisor chooses an exponent 0..a for p, 0..b for q and 0..c for r, so
// tau(n) = (a + 1)(b + 1)(c + 1). For 360 = 2^3 * 3^2 * 5 that gives
// 4 * 3 * 2 = 24. The script calls the builtin; it does not reimplement
// the count.
//
// Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
// ch. 16, section 16.7 (the divisor functions). ndivisors is exact.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/divisors/divisor-count.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/divisors/divisor-count.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// There is no knob: the sample numbers are the point of the script.
// ============================================================================

// ---- divisor counts -----------------------------------------------------------------
for x in {1, 12, 360, 100, 97} do ndivisors(x)

// ---- what the formula says -----------------------------------------------------------
// 1 has one divisor (itself); 12 = 2^2 * 3 gives (2 + 1)(1 + 1) = 6.
// 360 = 2^3 * 3^2 * 5 gives (3 + 1)(2 + 1)(1 + 1) = 24, the classic check.
// 100 = 2^2 * 5^2 gives 3 * 3 = 9 (odd: a square has an odd divisor count,
// because its divisors pair up except the square root).
// 97 is prime, so it has exactly 2 divisors.
print("tau(360) from the exponents:", (3 + 1) * (2 + 1) * (1 + 1))
print("ndivisors(360):", ndivisors(360))
print("tau(100) from the exponents:", (2 + 1) * (2 + 1))
print("ndivisors(100):", ndivisors(100))
// ---- expected output ----
// = {1, 6, 24, 9, 2}
// = tau(360) from the exponents: 24
// = ndivisors(360): 24
// = tau(100) from the exponents: 9
// = ndivisors(100): 9
